@startuml
!include ../../../tools/diagrams/kcna-theme.puml
title base64 is encoding, not encryption

rectangle "Secret db-creds\n<size:11>data:</size>\n<size:11>  password: c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQ=</size>\n<size:11>looks protected · is not</size>" <<accent>> as sec

rectangle "One command undoes it" <<groupBad>> as undo {
  rectangle "kubectl get secret db-creds -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d\n<size:11>→ supersecret</size>\n\n<size:11>base64 exists to make binary safe to put in YAML and JSON.</size>\n<size:11>It takes no key, so it protects nothing. It is not a cipher.</size>" <<bad>> as dec
  rectangle "Who can already read it\n<size:11>anyone with API access to get the Secret</size>\n<size:11>anyone with list or watch on Secrets — that reads EVERY Secret in the namespace</size>\n<size:11>anyone with access to etcd, where it is stored UNENCRYPTED by default</size>\n<size:11>anyone who can CREATE A POD in the namespace — mount it and print it</size>\n<size:11>any container running privileged: true on the node</size>" <<bad>> as who
  dec -[hidden]down-> who
}

rectangle "What actually protects a Secret" <<groupGood>> as prot {
  rectangle "1 · Encryption at Rest\n<size:11>an EncryptionConfiguration on the API server so etcd holds ciphertext</size>" <<good>> as p1
  rectangle "2 · RBAC, least privilege\n<size:11>no blanket get/list/watch on Secrets; remember creating Pods is indirect access</size>" <<good>> as p2
  rectangle "3 · Restrict to specific containers\n<size:11>mount a Secret only into the container that needs it</size>" <<good>> as p3
  rectangle "4 · External secret stores\n<size:11>Vault, a cloud KMS, the Secrets Store CSI driver — the cluster never holds the material</size>" <<good>> as p4
  p1 -[hidden]down-> p2
  p2 -[hidden]down-> p3
  p3 -[hidden]down-> p4
}

rectangle "What a Secret DOES buy you, even so\n<size:11>the value is out of the container image and out of git</size>\n<size:11>a Secret is only sent to a node if a Pod there needs it</size>\n<size:11>the kubelet holds mounted Secret data in tmpfs — RAM, never written to disk</size>\n<size:11>kubectl describe REDACTS the values; the type system and RBAC can treat it differently from config</size>" <<plain>> as buys

sec -down-> undo
sec -down-> prot
prot -[hidden]down-> buys

caption <i>The exam sentence: Secrets are NOT encrypted, they are ENCODED with base64. Knowing that is the point — a Secret is an intent-declaring container for sensitive values, not a vault.</i>\n<i>Everything that makes it actually secret is configuration you add around it.</i>
@enduml
